export const LESSONS={
scenarios:{title:'A model is a set of choices.',objective:'CH7-01 · Reproduce a compound path and identify the assumptions that created it.',body:`<p>A simulation calculates consequences of a specified model using repeatable pseudo-random draws. It does not turn assumptions into market evidence. Our original model starts at £100 and takes three hypothetical periods. Each period independently draws one of −a%, 0%, +a%, each with probability 1/3. The spread a is 5, 10 or 20. These are percentage returns, not price changes in pounds.</p><div class="formula">Next price = current price × (1 + return / 100)</div><p>Worked example with a=10: +10%, 0%, −10% gives £100→£110→£110→£99. The arithmetic mean return is zero, but the compounded change is −1%. Predict before calculating: do opposite returns cancel? Their multiplication, 1.1×.9=.99, shows they do not.</p><p>The model assumes independent draws, equal probabilities, a fixed distribution and no prices below zero. It bounds one-period losses to a%, excludes jumps beyond its three allowed outcomes, and omits taxes, trading, dividends and volatility clustering. Three model periods are not labelled years or days: no annualised forecast is implied. Change one assumption deliberately; log it even when the result looks worse.</p>`,reflection:'Show one compounded path and explain why equal model probabilities and bounded outcomes are assumptions rather than measured market facts.',criteria:'I used multiplication and named independence, bounded tails and fixed probabilities.'},
forward:{title:'Commit to buy. Keep both directions.',objective:'CH7-02 · Calculate the terminal payoff of one long forward unit.',body:`<p>A simplified long forward commits to buy one unit at a fixed delivery price K on the terminal date. At that date the asset is worth S<sub>T</sub>. The economic payoff is S<sub>T</sub>−K. A low terminal price produces a negative payoff: the obligation does not disappear.</p><div class="formula">Long forward terminal payoff = S<sub>T</sub> − K</div><p>Worked K=100: if terminal price is £120 the payoff is +£20; at £90 it is −£10; at £100 it is zero. Predict: is a forward floor of zero correct? No: that floor describes optional exercise for a call, not a binding long forward.</p><p>There is no initial premium, margin, financing, counterparty default or discounting in this teaching example. We calculate terminal payoff only; we are not determining a fair forward price. Real contracts can require collateral and involve credit or funding costs. Units are pounds per one underlying unit, never percentage investment returns.</p>`,reflection:'Compare terminal prices below and above strike. State the obligation, units and exclusions; do not call the positive side a guarantee.',criteria:'I kept negative forward payoffs and distinguished payoff from return and contract pricing.'},
call:{title:'The right has a price.',objective:'CH7-03 · Distinguish a call’s terminal payoff from undiscounted profit after premium.',body:`<p>A long call gives the right to buy one unit at strike K on the terminal date. The holder can choose not to exercise. Therefore payoff is max(S<sub>T</sub>−K,0). The premium is paid for this right and is excluded from payoff. To calculate our simplified undiscounted profit, subtract the premium once.</p><div class="formula">Call payoff = max(S<sub>T</sub> − K, 0)<br>Undiscounted profit = call payoff − premium</div><p>Worked K=100, premium £6: terminal £90 gives payoff £0 and profit −£6. Terminal £104 gives payoff £4 and profit −£2. Terminal £120 gives payoff £20 and profit £14. Predict: does finishing above strike guarantee profit? No: the payoff first has to recover the premium. Break-even is £106 under these assumptions.</p><p>The £6 premium is supplied, not priced by this simulation. We deliberately ignore the time value of money, so subtracting an earlier premium from terminal cash is an undiscounted teaching convention. Financing or discounting would change the economic comparison. No option-pricing derivation, early exercise, dividends, fees, taxes or hedging is assessed.</p>`,reflection:'Give a terminal price where the call is in the money but makes a loss. Distinguish pounds of payoff, premium and undiscounted profit.',criteria:'I subtract premium once from payoff and disclose the no-discounting convention.'},
interpret:{title:'Separate precision from credibility.',objective:'CH7-04 · Interpret simulated outcome counts without treating the model as a forecast.',body:`<p>A 100-path sample has sampling variation even when its generator is fixed. Reporting “37 of 100 calls made a profit” describes this run, conditional on its assumptions. It does not establish a 37% future market probability. Repeating the same seed exactly reproduces the path list; changing the seed changes draws, not the underlying assumptions.</p><p>More paths can reduce random simulation noise around this chosen model’s outputs. They cannot verify equal probabilities, independence, missing tail events or a fixed distribution. Predict: if we widen a from 10 to 20 with the seed fixed, will the signs of the individual returns change? No: the same draw chooses down, zero or up; only their size changes. Terminal outcomes and derivative payoffs can change nonlinearly.</p><p>For a=10, a three-down path is £100×.9³=£72.90. For a=20 it is £51.20. The model still excludes outcomes below that floor; markets do not promise such a bound. A minimum among simulated paths is neither a market worst case nor a risk guarantee. Review every path in the table rather than relying on an average alone.</p>`,reflection:'Explain a fixed-seed, changed-spread comparison and name a risk excluded by the model. Separate numerical reproducibility from external validity.',criteria:'I described conditional results and rejected forecast or worst-case guarantees.'}
};
